Q: How to capture old value and new value in INotifyPropertyChanged implementation of PropertyChanged event in C# A: <p>As @Andy mentioned, you can implement INotifyPropetyChanging to get the old value, but storing is a big cumbersome, so I made a helper class <a href="https://gist.github.com/ice1e0/d142dc0940dbf26ec27ec6da52e72cfe" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NotifyPropertyChangingCache</a> you can use as following:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>class EntityBase : INotifyPropertyChanged, INotifyPropertyChanging
{ /* ... */ }

// initiating the entity
var entity = new EntityBase();

// initiating the cache:
var notifyPropertyChangingCache = new Ice1e0.ComponentModel.NotifyPropertyChangingCache();
entity.PropertyChanging += notifyPropertyChangingCache.OnPropertyChanging;

// your property changed event looks like this
entity.PropertyChanged += (s, e) =&gt;
{
    var oldValue = notifyPropertyChangingCache.GetLastPropertyValue(e.PropertyName);

    // your code goes here ...
};

// when you are done with your entity, don't forget to remove the events (we don't want to create a memory leak ...)
entity.PropertyChanging -= notifyPropertyChangingCache.OnPropertyChanging;
//entity.PropertyChanged -= ... (I assume you use not a in-line method here as I did in this example)
</code></pre>

Q: Finding prime number in a range in javascript A: <pre><code>

//here is a very simple solution for prime numbers only.

for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 30; i++) {
    let prime = i &gt; 1;
    for(let j = 2; j &lt;= Math.sqrt(i); j++) {
        if (i % j == 0){
            prime = false;
            break;
        }
    }
    if (prime) {
        console.log(`Prime Numbers: ${i}`);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is the breakdown of how it works:</p>
<p><strong>Range Setup:</strong> The outer loop checks every integer from <strong>1 to 30</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Initial Rule:</strong> It defaults <code>prime</code> to <code>true</code> for any number greater than 1.</p>
<p><strong>Efficiency Filter:</strong> The inner loop tests potential divisors, but smartly stops at the <strong>square root of the number</strong> to save processing time.</p>
<p><strong>The Test:</strong> If it finds any divisor with a remainder of zero (<code>i % j == 0</code>), it marks the number as <strong>not prime</strong> and stops testing that number immediately.</p>
<p><strong>Output:</strong> Only numbers that successfully pass the test without failing are printed.</p>
